WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:04.633
We got the intel brief we got
about 30 days before we left.

00:00:04.633 --> 00:00:08.567
Said that you're going to
the safest place in Iraq.

00:00:08.567 --> 00:00:13.433
In April 2004, one year after
the fall of Saddam Hussein,

00:00:13.433 --> 00:00:17.333
Iraq was controlled by a
US-led transitional government.

00:00:17.333 --> 00:00:21.200
This period marked a relative
lull in violence in the war.

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.333
The soldiers stationed at
Camp War Eagle expected

00:00:24.333 --> 00:00:27.633
an uneventful deployment.

00:00:27.633 --> 00:00:29.633
When we deployed,
you know, we knew

00:00:29.633 --> 00:00:32.834
that there had been a
lot of enemy activity

00:00:32.834 --> 00:00:35.066
in Sadr City up to that point.

00:00:35.066 --> 00:00:37.400
You know, I think it
was general knowledge

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.000
that there hadn't been a shot
fired in over six months.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.100
The division was tasked with
peacekeeping and supporting

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:44.834
local rebuilding
efforts in a part

00:00:44.834 --> 00:00:46.734
of Baghdad known as Sadr City.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:53.433
Initially, what we thought we
were going to do is go in there

00:00:53.433 --> 00:00:57.300
and provide security to
contractors who are going

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:00.433
to come in and repair the
damaged sewage system,

00:01:00.433 --> 00:01:03.200
repair the damaged
freshwater system,

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:04.667
repair the electrical systems.

00:01:04.667 --> 00:01:06.900
All the things that
had deteriorated

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:08.767
since the first Gulf War,
because of the bombing

00:01:08.767 --> 00:01:11.800
that we did there and
because of neglect

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:15.734
by Saddam Hussein's government
before we finally toppled it.

00:01:15.734 --> 00:01:19.033
That was the mission that we
thought we were going to do.

00:01:19.033 --> 00:01:23.200
Sadr City was increasingly
controlled by Muqtada Al-Sadr,

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.433
an influential religious
activist and vocal opponent

00:01:26.433 --> 00:01:28.800
of foreign occupation in Iraq.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:32.367
On March 28, US coalition
forces ordered the closure

00:01:32.367 --> 00:01:36.166
of Al-Sadr's newspaper,
which escalated the tensions

00:01:36.166 --> 00:01:39.667
that would lead to violence.

00:01:39.667 --> 00:01:42.700
When the ambush started, we
were actually headed back in.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:44.800
We had been out
escorting the sewage

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:46.734
trucks throughout the day.

00:01:46.734 --> 00:01:49.567
As we moved in and moved down
that road, a couple of rounds

00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:50.633
fired.

00:01:50.633 --> 00:01:53.233
And we stopped to look
from where it was.

00:01:53.233 --> 00:01:56.533
And that's when it
just kept ramping up.

00:01:56.533 --> 00:01:58.066
And there was more
and more and more.

00:01:58.066 --> 00:02:01.000
Within a few minutes, we knew
exactly what was going on.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:02.133
We were involved
in a close ambush,

00:02:02.133 --> 00:02:03.433
and we needed to push through.

00:02:03.433 --> 00:02:07.333
And that's when the
fighting got real heavy.

